---
import SeoLayout from '../../layouts/SeoLayout.astro';
import { personaBySlug, isPublished, personaUrl, guideUrl } from '../../lib/seo-routes';
import { APP_URL } from '../../lib/site';

const persona = personaBySlug('internal-comms');
const title = 'Live translation for internal communications';
const description =
  'Translate the Q&A, not just the leadership presentation — and keep the translation in the ' +
  'recording, which Teams and Google Meet both drop.';
---

<SeoLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={personaUrl(persona.slug)}
  noindex={!isPublished(persona)}
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      An all-hands has a specific failure mode: the parts that are easy to translate are the parts
      that matter least. The prepared presentation could have been a written update. The Q&A is what
      makes it a town hall — and it is usually left in the working language.
    </p>

    <h2>Two things that decide whether it works</h2>

    <h3>Translate the questions, not only the answers</h3>
    <p>
      If leadership is translated into five languages and questions must come back in English, the
      meeting has told everyone whose language is the real one. It also means the questions you
      receive are filtered by English confidence rather than by importance.
    </p>

    <h3>Keep the translation in the recording</h3>
    <p>
      For a global company, the asynchronous audience is usually larger than the live one.
      Microsoft's own documentation states that Teams
      <strong
        >recordings capture only the original meeting audio and do not include interpretation audio</strong
      >, and that translated transcripts disappear when the meeting ends. Google Meet does not
      translate recordings or live streams either.
    </p>
    <p>
      If most of your company watches the next morning, that means the translation served the
      smaller half.
    </p>

    <h2>Why native tooling handles this format worst</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Teams Interpreter is unavailable in town halls and webinars</strong>, and requires
        Microsoft 365 Copilot
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google Meet supports one language pair per meeting</strong> — three offices in three languages
        cannot use it at all
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Zoom translates to captions only</strong>, so international staff read while
        everyone else listens
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we offer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Symmetric translation</strong>, so questions arrive in the asker's own language
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-track recording and per-language transcripts</strong> — the translation survives
        the meeting
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No per-language tariff</strong>, so adding a fifth office is not a fifth invoice
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When you should not choose us</h2>
    <p>
      For a works council consultation, a regulatory briefing, or anything people sign off on, book
      human interpreters. We are for the recurring internal sessions where the alternative today is
      that half the company follows along approximately.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('all-hands-meeting-global-team-languages')}
          >Running an all-hands for a global team across languages</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('international-town-hall-meeting-languages')}
          >How to run an international town hall across languages</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('include-non-native-english-speakers-in-meetings')}
          >How to include non-native English speakers in meetings</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta">
      <a href={APP_URL}>Try it on your next all-hands</a> — no contract, no minimum.
    </p>
  </article>
</SeoLayout>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  li {
    margin-block: 0.5rem;
  }
  .cta {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
